WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
zZooz

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:45.560
For millions of years, before birds evolved and long before the rise of the mammals, cold-blooded

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.760
animals ruled the world.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:59.680
In some places, they still do.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:10.400
Some of these reptiles witnessed the dinosaurs come and go.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:20.480
Yet in all that time, they themselves remained virtually unchanged.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.440
Among them were some of the most impressive reptiles alive today.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.800
They took that most characteristic of reptilian features to scale to extremes.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.840
They turned it into armour.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:47.440
That increased their weight, but nevertheless, some can still move with extraordinary speed.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:55.280
And although they may appear cold and impassive, they can nonetheless be passionate.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:03.840
And even affectionate.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.440
Among them are the biggest of all reptiles alive today.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:19.760
They're the crocodiles, the turtles and the tortoises.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:26.240
This is the giant Galapagos tortoise, and it's climbed all the way up the flank of

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.080
this great volcano, and it's here wandering around the rim of the crater.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.680
But why should it come to such a bleak and inhospitable place?

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:46.000
Tortoises, being reptiles, can't generate their own body heat internally, as we do.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.480
Instead, they must get it from their surroundings.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:55.280
And these particular ones have come up here to warm themselves on the hot volcanic rocks

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.280
among the jets of steam and sulphurous gas.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:09.000
They live longer than any other animal on Earth, well over 150 years.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.200
They weigh up to a quarter of a ton and have shells over a metre across.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:24.720
They really are giants.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:32.560
Having a body encased in shell obviously brings problems, and one of them is how do you mate?

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.000
Making love in a suit of armour is not easy.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:46.120
But the males have a very ingenious solution.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.880
The underside of their shell is concave, so it fits neatly over the domed top of the shell

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.280
of the female, who is somewhat smaller.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.120
That doesn't make clambering on top of her any easier initially, but once the male is

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:19.360
up there, it will reduce his chance of slipping off.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:28.160
The two shells fit together as neatly as two spoons.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:40.320
So mating can begin, and once started, it can go on for a long time.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.360
A tortoise's shell is so familiar to us, it's easy to forget what an extraordinary

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.800
construction it is.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.440
But how did it originate?

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:58.200
Nearly all reptiles are covered in scales, and so were the tortoise's ancestors.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:05.080
But then, as they evolved, a radical change took place.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:13.760
The ribs expanded outwards so that they enclosed the hip and the shoulder joints.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.200
They enlarged and fused with other bones beneath the skin.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.880
They widened, and eventually they joined together to form a bony box.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:32.960
Above, the scales in the skin enlarged to form a continuous shield of horn on the surface

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:41.240
of the box, and the basic armour was complete.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:47.600
By developing a shell, tortoises inevitably sacrifice speed, so they can't sprint off

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.400
and take shelter in a crack when danger threatens.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:58.440
But with a shell like that, they seldom need to.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:03.680
Some have added deluxe features to the basic model.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:15.240
This is an eastern box turtle.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:22.600
In North America, where it lives, there are plenty of would-be predators, raccoons among

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:34.160
them, and they have very nimble paws.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:41.440
But these turtles don't have to worry, because their shell has a special safety feature.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:47.840
It has a drawbridge, and when danger threatens, the box turtle just retreats into its shell

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:58.800
and pulls it up.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:19.800
The drawbridge fits so tightly, there is no crack for the raccoon to get its teeth into.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:28.240
After a while, most raccoons give up.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.480
Of course, when your head is inside your shell, you can't see whether your attacker has gone

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:43.400
or not, so it's best to check before you emerge fully.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:48.920
A shell is an excellent defence against predators, but there are other dangers against which

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:55.120
it provides no protection whatever.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:01.800
It's midsummer, it's nearly midday, and it's very, very hot, and as a consequence, I'm

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:02.800
sweating.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:09.400
Sweating is a direct response to heat that only mammals can do.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:15.160
No reptile like this gopher tortoise has got sweat glands, but instead it's got another

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:20.240
way of keeping itself cool.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.000
Most reptiles head for the shade when it gets too hot.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:31.040
To see where this gopher tortoise is heading here in Florida, I'm going to use this.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:36.960
A remotely controlled mini camera on wheels with its own lights.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:40.960
It can go pretty well anywhere.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.200
The gopher tortoise is heading for home.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:58.800
And with luck, I'll be able to follow it, the tortoise, as it goes down into its burrow.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.680
And that camera has also got a thermometer mounted on it.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.840
Already I can see the temperature is beginning to drop.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:14.520
The further down the burrow we go, the cooler it gets.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.480
Now, where's the tortoise?

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.160
There it is.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:26.840
I'm right behind.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:33.560
We're now a couple of meters in, but the burrow could go on for some 50 feet, 17 meters.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:42.040
And it's all been built by this tortoise.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:50.920
Well that is not a tortoise, that's a rattlesnake.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:58.960
Obviously taking shelter from the heat just as the tortoise is.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:04.000
The tortoise is so well-armoured it's in no danger from the snake, and gopher tortoises

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:12.680
don't seem to mind sharing their burrows.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:18.920
Changed its mind and there's its rattle.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:24.720
Over a hundred different species of animal have been recorded taking shelter inside tortoises

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.720
tunnels.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.680
In fact, some can live nowhere else.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:36.080
There's another reason why tortoises' homes are so popular.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:42.920
Tortoises are one of the few animals here that can actually dig.

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:47.480
Each may have more than one burrow within its territory, and that's very valuable because

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:56.840
they have to deal with an even greater danger than sunstroke.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:04.240
Bushfires.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:23.080
These are a major and recurrent threat to all the animals that live here.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:40.080
Tortoises' tunnels are invaluable places in which to take refuge.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.360
Gopher tortoises may seem to be unassuming creatures, but their engineering skills are

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:53.480
essential for the survival of the whole ecosystem.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:12:06.720
The ground is still smoking, but the flames have passed and the emergency is over.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:12.800
So by digging tunnels, tortoises save not only their lives, but the lives of hundreds

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:15.360
of other animals.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:20.640
But tunnels aren't the only place where you can escape extremes of temperature.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:27.200
There's another environment that's cooler and even more stable.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.440
Water.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:40.960
Some of the ancestral tortoises started to spend all their time there and became turtles.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:51.080
Some still walk slowly along the bottom in much the same way as their ancestors walked

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:55.080
on land.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.800
Water is a good place for a cold-blooded animal to live.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.000
It retains its warmth through the night and stays comfortably cool during the heat of

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.160
the day.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:24.000
So turtles are able to keep their body temperature relatively constant without much difficulty.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:36.600
Many have developed webs between their toes and have become very efficient swimmers.

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:42.000
The most aquatic of all freshwater turtles is found in New Guinea and a few rivers like

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:47.160
this one in northern Australia.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:52.640
The pig-nosed turtle.

00:13:52.640 --> 00:14:00.200
Its feet have become completely transformed into flippers and are of little use on land.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:08.380
And indeed, the pig-nosed turtle rarely comes ashore.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:13.400
But turtles are descended from land-living ancestors, and so they still need to breathe

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:18.400
air.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:31.360
Females also have to return to land in order to lay their eggs.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:38.720
Pig-nosed nest during the dry season high up on the riverbank.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.840
If turtle eggs get wet, the babies inside them will drown.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:48.240
At least that is the case with most turtles.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:54.920
An egg for a turtle represents a huge investment, as it does for any reptile.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:15:01.440
So turtles go through a great deal of trouble to make sure that they lay their eggs in safe,

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.640
dry places.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:08.440
So you would think that dropping one into water would be a disaster.

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:09.440
But watch.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:39.160
A fully developed baby turtle.

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:46.680
And it's come from an egg which, as far as we know, is unique in the reptile world.

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:53.080
It cannot only survive being flooded, it actually requires to be submerged in water in order

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.800
to hatch.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:01.200
This enables the pig-nosed to make the hatching of its eggs coincide with the onset of the

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:03.440
rainy season.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:14.240
A view inside the egg would show the babies to be fully developed.

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:30.120
They can remain there in a kind of suspended animation, if necessary, for weeks.

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:35.600
When the rains finally arrive, they are torrential.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:52.480
The river rises swiftly and soon the nests are flooded.

00:16:52.480 --> 00:17:00.240
This would be a disaster for most turtles, but the unhatched pig-noses are ready for

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:01.240
it.

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:23.080
The moment they'd been waiting for.

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:28.640
Their unique waiting strategy ensures that no matter how late the rains are, the young

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:36.760
turtles only emerge when the rivers are full and there's plenty to eat.

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:47.560
They're able to swim immediately.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:52.840
In due course, the females among them will return here to lay eggs themselves.

00:17:52.840 --> 00:18:02.160
The males, however, will never set foot on dry land again.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:08.600
No turtles are better suited to life in freshwater than the pig-nose, but the most extreme adaptations

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:20.920
for swimming are found in those turtles that went to sea.

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:26.840
Marine turtles have altered their front legs really radically and turned them into oar-like

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:31.840
flippers.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:38.040
They're so at home in the sea, they even mate while swimming.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:46.040
A male turtle has special hooks on his front flippers that enable him to cling onto the

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:52.240
female's shell, and he has to have a firm grip, for she makes no allowance for him as

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:57.300
she swims.

00:18:57.300 --> 00:19:02.760
But hanging onto his female is going to get much harder for this male.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:09.280
A rival has appeared.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:15.240
The male's armour protects most of his body, but his rear flippers are exposed, and they

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:34.040
are relatively soft and vulnerable.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:38.440
There can be little doubt that this hurts, but there's not much that the first male can

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:40.800
do about it.

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:53.640
If he lets go with even a single flipper, he will lose his grip, and his female.

00:19:53.640 --> 00:20:11.840
The rival tries again and attacks the front flipper.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:17.640
And now the male's troubles are about to double.

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:28.680
A second rival arrives.

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:43.840
The two challengers join forces and attack the male from both sides.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:51.480
Its only option is to grin and bear it.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:54.400
Now a third hopeful male joins in.

00:20:54.400 --> 00:21:12.280
The female tries to shake them off, but there's no shifting them.

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:14.640
Things are going from bad to worse.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:21.140
Even more males gang up on the hapless couple.

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:28.040
Some of the gang try to force themselves between the mating pair.

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:40.160
The pair have now been submerged for a long time, and both of them are in desperate need

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:43.000
of a breath.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:47.640
If the rivals can prevent the male from reaching the surface, he will have no choice but to

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:48.640
let go.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:22:05.280
He's in real danger of drowning.

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:19.080
At last, the determined couple break free and make a dash for the surface.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:36.040
With a welcome gasp of air, the pair escape.

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:50.080
One by one, the gang give up.

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:55.160
It was over 200 million years ago that the first turtles took to the water, but they

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.120
were not alone.

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:09.360
Another group of reptiles were also making the same move, and they too were armored giants.

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:28.320
Crocodilians, like turtles and tortoises, have barely changed since the time of the

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:29.320
dinosaurs.

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:36.960
Today, crocodiles, caiman and alligators live in tropical waters throughout the world.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:43.400
Tortoises and crocodiles are obviously very different, but they do have one thing in common

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:46.080
– armor.

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:54.200
Their bodies are encased by tough, thick scales, particularly along the back.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:58.520
In tortoises, that armor is clearly defensive.

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:03.200
But for crocodiles, it has an extra function.

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:08.520
Just below each of these scales lies a network of blood vessels.

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:12.920
A crocodile can control the flow of the blood within them.

00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:19.080
When basking, it allows it to circulate freely, so transferring the sun's warmth from these

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:23.000
ridged scales to the rest of its body.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:33.320
The crocodile, in short, has rows of very effective solar panels all down its back.

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:39.700
And a sophisticated solar heating system like that is a very valuable facility for a cold-blooded

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:48.340
creature.

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:53.120
Being cold-blooded brings considerable advantages to a crocodile.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:58.880
Whereas a warm-blooded predator, like a lion, would die if it didn't feed every few days,

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:03.920
a crocodile, if necessary, can go without food for months on end.

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:08.880
And that means that crocodiles can live in places where no warm-blooded predator could

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:15.920
survive and wait for events that only happen two or three times each year.

00:25:15.920 --> 00:25:23.480
And one of those events will occur tonight, right here.

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:37.040
This road in northern Australia is close to the coast, and it floods at the highest tides.

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:42.360
Night falls, and the scene changes dramatically.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:52.480
The road is now covered in water and crocodiles.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:59.200
But this is no random gathering.

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:07.880
The crocodiles are all here for a reason.

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:17.560
We know that some of them have travelled over 60 miles, 100 kilometres, to get here.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:40.920
But how they know when to come here, we have little idea.

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:48.840
There must be some 40 crocodiles assembled in the river behind me.

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:55.280
And what makes that sight all the more remarkable is that these are saltwater crocodiles, which

00:26:55.280 --> 00:27:00.600
are normally very territorial and intolerant of one another.

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:05.520
So there must be something pretty special happening in the river tonight.

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:08.360
And indeed, there is.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:14.640
This river is tidal, but it's been crossed by a barrage.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:22.360
However, at particularly high tides, the water flows over the barrage.

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:29.800
And that is the moment that all these crocodiles are waiting for.

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:33.160
With the saltwater come fish.

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:37.080
Mullet have been waiting for weeks to migrate up the river to breed.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:42.880
This high tide is their first chance to cross the barrage.

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:53.680
And the crocodiles are waiting for them.

00:27:53.680 --> 00:28:00.560
Remember, it's now pitch dark.

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:06.440
Our infrared cameras give us a clear view, but the crocodiles can see virtually nothing.

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:29.640
So they wait with open jaws, ready to snap them shut at the first touch of a fish.

00:28:29.640 --> 00:28:49.280
Normally saltwater crocodiles would not tolerate being so close to each other.

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:55.200
They do compete for the best fishing spots, but their disputes are settled with the minimum

00:28:55.200 --> 00:29:11.520
of fuss.

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:15.280
The fish keep coming for over an hour.

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:26.240
But as the tide starts to fall, so their numbers dwindle.

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:33.480
With so many crocodiles competing, some inevitably go hungry.

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:44.280
But for those that stay around, there will be a second bite to this particular cherry.

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:56.520
The next high tide comes during the day and brings yet more fish.

00:29:56.520 --> 00:30:08.160
The crocodiles can now see the fish, but that doesn't seem to make them any easier to catch.

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:26.480
These skillful hunters are surely dramatic proof that reptiles are certainly not simple

00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:28.680
minded creatures.

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:33.160
They've predicted the time of the arrival of the fish with astonishing accuracy, and

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:57.280
they have worked out just what they have to do to catch them.

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:02.800
They have also managed to suppress their normal antagonism to one another, so that they could

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:14.600
all take advantage of this bonanza.

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:32.760
The more we learn about crocodiles, the more we realize what complex creatures they are.

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:39.320
Despite what you might think, crocodilians are among the most talkative of reptiles and

00:31:39.320 --> 00:31:40.320
amphibians.

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:46.960
Indeed, they're second only to the frogs in the variety of noises that they make.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:52.720
The most impressive sounds of all come from the American alligator.

00:31:52.720 --> 00:32:17.440
When the breeding season starts, the males begin to proclaim their ownership of terraces.

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:44.960
The vibrations in his body are so powerful they make the water dance along his back.

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:49.280
The alligator travels through water even better than it does through air, and he can be heard

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:53.520
by other alligators hundreds of meters away.

00:32:53.520 --> 00:33:01.080
This is a clear statement of ownership of territory.

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:06.360
That's fine when he does it lying in his own patch, but watch what happens when he bellows

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:25.840
close to another male.

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:44.200
But the rivals don't come to blows.

00:33:44.200 --> 00:33:50.280
They sort out their differences with gestures.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:56.040
Head slaps and gaping jaws are very obvious signals, but alligators also send messages

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:58.640
in less conspicuous ways.

00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:03.920
Raising their backs slightly above the surface of the water is a significant move.

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:09.920
It's a claim to dominance.

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:15.520
Using signals that are almost imperceptible to us, all these individuals are sending messages

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:24.480
to each other, making claim and counterclaim.

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:31.200
Communication between alligators can be very subtle and quiet, but there are some occasions

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:37.480
when they really want to make their meaning very unambiguously clear, and one of those

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:47.200
is when they're guarding their nests, as this one is.

00:34:47.200 --> 00:34:59.400
I think that was pretty clear.

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:08.920
Anyway, I won't press the point.

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:15.240
Communication between crocodiles starts even before they've hatched.

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:28.400
A tranquil pool in Argentina, and in it a female broad-snouted caiman.

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:34.200
She laid her eggs in a pile of vegetation close to the water almost three months ago.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:37.520
Now sounds are coming from it.

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:44.360
The eggs are beginning to hatch.

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:52.960
Even while the eggs are still within the nest, their mother can hear them from some way away.

00:35:52.960 --> 00:36:14.320
Back on the nest, she listens intently.

00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:34.800
Then, very gently, she starts to take it apart.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:48.440
She can't know exactly where each of her babies is, and stops every few seconds to listen.

00:36:48.440 --> 00:37:00.000
At last, the young are free.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:10.400
But she doesn't abandon them.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:19.920
She is going to take them down to the pool that she's selected as their nursery.

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:25.040
Some babies start to make the journey for themselves, but they continue to call, and

00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:39.240
that helps their mother locate them.

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:45.000
Caiman jaws are among the strongest in the animal kingdom, but now she uses hers with

00:37:45.000 --> 00:38:12.520
the greatest delicacy and gentleness.

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:25.680
So at last, her babies are brought together in the nursery pool.

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:31.560
But their mother's job is still not finished.

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:40.840
Several of the eggs have failed to hatch.

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:51.680
One by one, she takes these in her mouth.

00:38:51.680 --> 00:38:54.600
The shell around the egg is quite strong.

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:59.200
The babies must make a considerable effort to free themselves, and for some, it's more

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:02.320
than they can manage.

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:20.480
So she starts to crack the unhatched egg with her teeth.

00:39:20.480 --> 00:39:46.200
Once the hard shell has been broken away, she has to pierce the leathery in a membrane.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:40:03.800
Without her help, this baby might not have hatched at all.

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:09.020
But the female Caiman's maternal duties are not over even now.

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:15.640
She will stand guard over her babies for the next few months.

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:21.960
All crocodilians take good care of their young, but one of them takes parental responsibilities

00:40:21.960 --> 00:40:28.160
to a really astonishing level.

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:42.120
The Janos of Venezuela, a lush wetland teeming with wildlife of all kinds.

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:56.400
As well as birds, there are amphibious rodents, capybara, and a spectacle caiman.

00:40:56.400 --> 00:41:04.680
This female is looking after an extraordinarily large number of babies.

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:14.960
But most of these are not in fact hers.

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:19.660
This nursery pool is being used by several caiman families.

00:41:19.660 --> 00:41:24.080
But instead of all the mothers staying nearby, one of them has taken charge of the whole

00:41:24.080 --> 00:41:31.080
creche.

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:35.200
Keeping an eye on all these energetic babies is not easy.

00:41:35.200 --> 00:41:51.280
There are lots of enemies around.

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:55.940
When danger threatens, the babies all run for protection to the female, even though

00:41:55.940 --> 00:42:02.640
she may not be their mother.

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:17.400
There are so many of them that there's not enough room for them all on her back.

00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:26.160
Soon these babies will face another hazard, and one that's not quite so easy to escape

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:27.160
from.

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:31.760
The water that has kept them safe until now is beginning to dry up.

00:42:31.760 --> 00:42:38.680
Each year, in the space of a few short months, the Janos is transformed from a flooded paradise

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:41.400
to a baking oven.

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:59.280
For some babies, the unrelenting heat and the lack of water has already been too much.

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:05.400
Any babies that are left alive will certainly die if they stay here.

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:28.920
So the mother decides to leave.

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:35.040
Calling to her creche, she sets off across the parched land.

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:58.840
In one long cavalcade, they march onwards in search of permanent water.

00:43:58.840 --> 00:44:02.200
For the mother, this is certainly exhausting.

00:44:02.200 --> 00:44:16.680
For her babies, with their tiny legs, it must be a real marathon.

00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:26.400
Some start to fall behind, but she stops.

00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:28.680
The babies call constantly.

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:33.640
She knows exactly where they all are, and waits until every single one of them has caught

00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:57.640
up.

00:44:57.640 --> 00:45:22.440
Only when all are with her will she set off again.

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:35.000
At last, safety.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:51.200
The babies are close to exhaustion.

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:58.760
Without such devotion from the female, few, if any of these baby caiman, would have survived.

00:45:58.760 --> 00:46:12.400
And remarkably, most of them aren't even her own.

00:46:12.400 --> 00:46:17.480
When parental care was first described in crocodiles, the reports were dismissed as

00:46:17.480 --> 00:46:24.480
too extraordinary to be true.

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:34.280
We may call reptiles cold-blooded, but they can show great tenderness.

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:56.600
Mammals and amphibians are full of surprises.

00:46:56.600 --> 00:47:08.160
They can look after their young with as much care as many a mammal.

00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:16.720
Their displays can be as colourful as that of any bird.

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:33.760
And they can astonish and enthrall us.

00:47:33.760 --> 00:47:39.760
Reptiles and amphibians are sometimes seen as simple, primitive creatures.

00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:42.440
That's a long way from the truth.

00:47:42.440 --> 00:47:48.760
The fact that they're solar powered means that their bodies require only 10% of the energy

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:52.840
that mammals of a similar size require.

00:47:52.840 --> 00:47:59.440
At a time when we ourselves are becoming increasingly concerned about the way in which we get our

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:05.160
energy from the environment and the wasteful way in which we use it, maybe there are things

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:32.000
that we can learn from life in cold blood.

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:45.180
We filmed a lot of different reptiles and amphibians during the making of this series.

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:49.800
We were looking for extraordinary behaviour, preferably for things that had never been

00:48:49.800 --> 00:49:01.340
filmed before.

00:49:01.340 --> 00:49:11.080
To see such wonders, we needed the help of scientists who were working in the field.

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:16.000
They passed on to us their insights and their discoveries and then they helped us to interpret

00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:18.440
the footage that we'd shot.

00:49:18.440 --> 00:49:21.040
I learnt a lot and had a lot of fun.

00:49:21.040 --> 00:49:26.780
But I was also alarmed to discover just how rare some of the subjects of our series have

00:49:26.780 --> 00:49:32.320
now become.

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:35.640
One of our key locations was the Galapagos Islands.

00:49:35.640 --> 00:49:39.160
Here giant tortoises were going to be among our stars.

00:49:39.160 --> 00:49:45.280
The scientists working for the Galapagos National Parks care for the wild animal populations,

00:49:45.280 --> 00:49:50.080
but they also look after one extraordinary, unique individual with whom I had a special

00:49:50.080 --> 00:49:52.400
appointment.

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:57.600
This is the rarest living animal in all the world.

00:49:57.600 --> 00:50:00.240
There is none rarer.

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:04.000
This is Lonesome George.

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:10.600
He's about the same age as I am, but his story starts a very long time ago.

00:50:10.600 --> 00:50:18.400
In the 17th century, when human beings first came to the Galapagos, there were about 15

00:50:18.400 --> 00:50:25.800
different kinds of giant tortoise, each living on its own island or its own great volcano

00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:30.000
isolated by unpassable lava flows.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.420
There are 13 large islands in the Galapagos and many smaller ones and they differ in both

00:50:37.420 --> 00:50:40.400
age and their vegetation.

00:50:40.400 --> 00:50:44.960
The tortoises differ too because their shells have evolved into the different shapes best

00:50:44.960 --> 00:50:48.960
suited for eating the food available on their own particular island.

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:54.040
On islands where there's abundant food on the ground, the tortoises have dome-shaped

00:50:54.040 --> 00:50:59.160
shells and short necks that only need reach downwards.

00:50:59.160 --> 00:51:03.840
But on islands where the tortoises browse on higher bushes, their necks are longer and

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:10.480
the shells are saddle-shaped at the front so they can stretch their necks upwards.

00:51:10.480 --> 00:51:16.400
When the first ships arrived here, there were thousands of each kind of tortoise.

00:51:16.400 --> 00:51:22.360
But then people began to slaughter the tortoises for meat.

00:51:22.360 --> 00:51:30.440
They discovered the remarkable fact that these creatures could live for a year without water

00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:32.280
or food.

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:37.560
So they took them on board their ships and slaughtered them at sea.

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:42.520
The tortoises on Pinter Island were apparently exterminated.

00:51:42.520 --> 00:51:52.920
But then in 1971 it was discovered that there was one lonely single survivor.

00:51:52.920 --> 00:51:58.840
That was Lonesome George.

00:51:58.840 --> 00:52:03.200
This film was taken over 30 years ago by the team that brought George back to the Charles

00:52:03.200 --> 00:52:05.360
Darwin Research Station.

00:52:05.360 --> 00:52:09.360
The scientists hoped that another Pinter tortoise might be discovered in some corner of his

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:12.600
island or even in a zoo somewhere in the world.

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:19.640
But none has ever been found.

00:52:19.640 --> 00:52:25.800
So now George lives in his own enclosure, completely safe but entirely by himself.

00:52:25.800 --> 00:52:28.560
He's the last of his kind.

00:52:28.560 --> 00:52:31.960
It's better news for the other Galapagos tortoises.

00:52:31.960 --> 00:52:37.480
Felipe Cruz from the research station showed me some of the work being done there.

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:42.560
They take eggs laid by wild tortoises and put them in incubators.

00:52:42.560 --> 00:52:47.120
The hatchlings are about the size of apples and have soft shells, so are vulnerable to

00:52:47.120 --> 00:52:55.320
predators, especially rats that were accidentally introduced to the Galapagos.

00:52:55.320 --> 00:52:59.280
The young ones I saw were only a few months old.

00:52:59.280 --> 00:53:03.800
They're kept in special enclosures and given all the foods they need to enable them to

00:53:03.800 --> 00:53:08.320
develop hard protective shells.

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:12.600
It takes a few months for their shells to harden and it's five years before they're

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:21.360
totally predator-proof.

00:53:21.360 --> 00:53:25.720
So far in total we have repatriated over 3,000 tortoises.

00:53:25.720 --> 00:53:26.720
3,000?

00:53:26.720 --> 00:53:29.840
There will be different islands.

00:53:29.840 --> 00:53:35.200
Scientists are also helping to solve another man-made problem.

00:53:35.200 --> 00:53:40.560
Domestic goats that have run wild are eating the tortoises' food and destroying the precious

00:53:40.560 --> 00:53:43.400
plant cover that they need for shade.

00:53:43.400 --> 00:53:55.040
So a systematic program of eradication has started.

00:53:55.040 --> 00:53:58.440
I was able to see the effects of this program for myself.

00:53:58.440 --> 00:54:03.200
We visited one island where two years earlier the goats had been eliminated and the difference

00:54:03.200 --> 00:54:04.560
was dramatic.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:08.640
The lush vegetation had returned.

00:54:08.640 --> 00:54:13.920
Now the tortoises can find the shade that is so important for them and has plenty of

00:54:13.920 --> 00:54:24.000
grass for them to eat.

00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:27.200
Dinos are not alone in being under threat.

00:54:27.200 --> 00:54:31.520
The amphibians, if anything, are in even greater danger.

00:54:31.520 --> 00:54:36.040
Not since the disappearance of the dinosaurs has a whole group of the animal kingdom been

00:54:36.040 --> 00:54:38.480
under such threat.

00:54:38.480 --> 00:54:44.120
In Japan, one of the most dramatic of amphibians, the giant salamander, has fewer and fewer

00:54:44.120 --> 00:54:51.080
places to live.

00:54:51.080 --> 00:54:54.360
In Panama, we filmed the golden frog.

00:54:54.360 --> 00:54:59.760
Since we took this shot, the species has become so rare that the few survivors have been caught

00:54:59.760 --> 00:55:06.440
to be protected in zoos, so it's now extinct in the wild.

00:55:06.440 --> 00:55:11.560
The gharials that we filmed with their babies were nearly exterminated in the 1970s when

00:55:11.560 --> 00:55:17.160
they lost most of their natural habitat and they're not safe yet.

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:22.200
To try and halt their decline, their eggs are being collected, hatched in incubators

00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:27.340
and the babies reared in captivity until they can be released in the wild, so there may

00:55:27.340 --> 00:55:30.560
be hope for them yet.

00:55:30.560 --> 00:55:34.320
The gopher tortoise we filmed in Florida is also in trouble.

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:38.840
The areas where it digs its burrows have become prime real estate and are now much sought

00:55:38.840 --> 00:55:43.120
after for building and farming.

00:55:43.120 --> 00:55:54.120
By explaining their problems to landowners and developers, they may yet have a future.

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:58.600
In the great island of Madagascar, there are more species of chameleon than in all the

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:03.360
rest of the world put together.

00:56:03.360 --> 00:56:08.160
But the destruction of the island's forests began centuries ago and only a few patches

00:56:08.160 --> 00:56:09.160
are left.

00:56:09.160 --> 00:56:14.240
They too are still being felled and chameleon species may be lost even before they've been

00:56:14.240 --> 00:56:20.720
identified.

00:56:20.720 --> 00:56:26.360
We will need to act now if we're not to lose what remains to us of the reptiles and amphibians

00:56:26.360 --> 00:56:31.320
that have survived for 200 million years.

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:37.040
Lonesome George, it seems, is doomed to be the last of his kind.

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:44.760
But at least he can be a living inspiration for us all to protect the remainder of the

00:56:44.760 --> 00:57:07.960
reptiles and amphibians of the world.

00:57:07.960 --> 00:57:24.280
We're too happy to share our staircase now.

00:57:54.280 --> 00:57:56.340
you
